00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.030 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon  michelle introduced me as former student  of clyde  but i think  it's fair to say i i am lifetime student  of clyde  student of  clyde herzmann school of thought school  of education which was one of a kind  i want to  say how honored i feel to be part of  this very important day  to pay my tribute  to the scholar one of a kind scholar  who  graced our careers and our lives  with his  remarkable body of knowledge he held and  he generated  but also with his simple and warm  friendship  today i want to show you a glimpse of  international clyde herzmann in  continuing what michael has already  showed and i want to continue on that  line  uh the project i want to talk to you  about is in a way continuation of  uh our whole  social work on social determinants of  health  during the time that who said  commissioned michael to set up the  social determinants of health the  imminent early learning partnership  acted as the knowledge network for  ecd in this capacity we undertook two  years of in extensive research to do  global environmental scan to identify  policies programs that are working that  are benefiting or counterproductive to  ecd then compile a report and inform  politicians and policy makers on how can  we use ecd this potent social  determinant of health not only enhance  the state of lives of children but also  enhance the population health and make  health a much more  equitable commodity to  attenuate if not eliminate the gradient  the shark gradient that everybody has  been talking about today we see the ses  gradient that we see in distribution of  health  in this capacity we put the report  together to commissioner  and then the commissioner expert of  except excuse me our report  appeared in commissioner's  report as chapter 5 equity from this  start  in this chapter in this report we made  number of recommendations  and  some of the recommendations that are  very  relevant to the project that i'm going  to be talking about were such as  to enhance the state of ecd ecd related  processes need to be monitored and  measured against set of internationally  agreed upon standards  that  building ecd promoting initiatives  around the existing capacities or even  obligations of the states or nations  makes them more  efficient and more  sustainable  and as the  impact of ecd goes well beyond the  borders so should be our efforts we need  to get together pull our resources and  create global alliances to work on ecd  and ecd matters  while this was happening in who  something else was happening in another  un agency  at the committee on the rights of the  child un committee on the rights of the  child something equally significant  seemingly separate  and it took a brilliant mind like clyde  hertzman to connect the dots  the committee on the rights of the child  is the monitoring body of the convention  on the rights of the child the  convention on the rights of the child  was adopted by general assembly in 1989  in recognition of the fact that the  charter of human rights is not adequate  to protect children  overwhelming number of countries right  away ratified the convention by today  193 so you can see the political will  behind this  and by virtue of this ratification they  have to a review their domestic laws and  framework and administrative process to  make sure that nothing is  counterproductive nothing is against  convention everything is aligned with  the principles of convention and be  every five years to report on the state  of children's right in their states  so from 1989 to 2000 the committee had  reviewed tens of reports and had seen  enough and had come to an alarming  observation that although convention  article one of the convention clearly  defines child as any individual below  the age of 18 but most of the reports  coming to the committee are talking  about older children are overlooking the  younger cohort the early years and that  was unacceptable for many reasons on top  of  all the fact that this was hitting the  convention right at the heart of it  the principle of non-discrimination is  the premise of the convention  so  in 2005 the committee took a day of  discussion and analyze this issue to  death and produce a resource and guiding  document called general comment number  seven how to implement convention for  young children  in 2000  late 2005 early 2006 over a fateful  dinner in new york city clyde sat around  the table with number of people  including a gentleman who is still our  collaborator to stay from the committee  on the rights of the child and he  started talking how unsatisfied  committee is with this  with the way nations handle this general  comment it's not serving the way it's  supposed to be it's theoretical etc and  there clyde  light went on  he looked at it and he said this is our  own recommendations in a nutshell here  is a pcd processes that needs to be  monitored here is the processes that  already builds up on the existing  obligation of a nation report writing  obligation and it's a process that needs  global alliance needs  big developmental agencies and child  right advocates to come together and  pull this  so he approached committee with  representatives of unicef and who and  committee took few months and wrote to  us  and invited us to ask  us as in how humanly learning  partnership to act as the secretary of a  project put together an ad hoc group and  start working on operationalizing  general common seven  the argue clyde had put to committee was  that this is a comprehensive wholesome  document prepared in all good intention  but remains  underutilized we just know how to do it  we have to operationalize and make it  easy to use  this is the group we put together help  acted as  as the  secretariat number of un agencies number  of international ngos and then in 2000  by 2008 we had the draft ready and we  showed to the committee the tool we  developed basically was a set of  indicators 17 set of indicators each one  designed to address one particular right  relevant to childhood from right to  breastfeeding to right to play etc all  of the indicators had the same generic  structure they all ask three sets of  questions structural questions what are  the policies and laws and right to  support of that right process questions  what are the programs and initiatives to  act upon the policies that are in place  and finally outcome  what has happened to the children or the  environment of that right as a result of  this structure and processes in place  and this is example of one of the  indicators every time the answer is no  we provide suggestions such as if there  is no initiative to raise awareness  against violence parenthood positive  parenthood  education in service education so on and  then we provide links to these programs  so the countries do not have to start  from  scratch  following the uh completion of the  indicators we received letter from the  committee on the rights of the child  encouraging us to pilot these in  different countries the first country we  went was sub-saharan tanzania  it was a quite successful  but because of expectedly because of  being resource poor country the outcome  indicators were notoriously low we got  150 about 150 evidence of  processes and structures in place in  support of right then we  digitized the tool span cloned it to  spanish version and went to chile chile  pilot was beyond and above our  expectation  it was majorly because this was a pure  and equal partnership between us and  unicef tanzania unicef san diego  ministry of children and unicef  san diego and minister of children of  the country  the pilots cumulatively showed us that  after we digitized the tool we changed  the name to indicators of early  childhood rights and then we launched  the two to the public in 2012  20th of november in recognition of the  day of university of children  the two pilots really showed that this  the mere exercise of using this right  tool is becomes like inventory taking  the governments understand what is in  place what is not and how can they go on  filling the gap in capacity they use  evidence for advocacy and their for  their action plan  and additionally it showed that  carefully designed and  piloted to such as this can really bring  the stakeholders to the table  keep them engaged can  create responsiveness in the government  in a timely manner because the timeline  is every five years report and the  countries have to show some progress at  least in the area of the rights within  that time frame  it can create transparency because that  speaks to itself and equity and  inclusiveness as they are rooted in the  human rights treaties human rights  treaties that are built upon  this non-discrimination  it can be it can  create consensus-oriented  decisions because everybody is involved  from the beginning and they take  ownership on what the data is showing  ownership in the data and promote  accountability  all of which are  pillars to good governance all of which  will enable states  social institutions governments as the  major duty bearers on the convention on  the rights of the child to  play their role more efficiently to  enhance the environments of children  and to  help children to achieve their  developmental maximum developmental  outcome  as we heard today from most of these  speakers and as most of us have  heard from clyde the reason for that is  simple  environments speak to genes and the  genes listen to them so we better make  sure they have something good to  talk about  thank you  you

